# Prospective independent Sketch alignment review

Candidate execution waits for the parent's final source freeze. Seven pinned Excalidraw 0.18.1 files at commit `a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84`, their MIT license, and fourteen current production baseline hashes are authenticated. Preparation attempt 1 exposed an absent author baseline snapshot for shared `lib/storage.mjs`; source/raw remain preserved. Attempt 2 verifies the same baseline hash directly against current production if the author snapshot is still absent. This custody difference is explicit; it is not an application defect.

Fresh planned fixtures cover six alignments, both distribution axes, signed lines and freehand point extrema, nested outer groups with partial selection, group-ID/shape-ID collisions, three distinct negative-gap overlap orders, coordinate-overflow atomicity, malformed selection/action, input immutability, genuine native unknown/opaque metadata, mixed editable/noneditable groups, inbound/outbound bindings, deleted native records, no-op undo/redo and current Save persistence where meaningful. Assertions will bind to the final documented API; no candidate implementation is read for acceptance before freeze.

The independent original oracle executes five literal source declarations after explicit TypeScript type erasure: `getMaximumGroups`, `getCommonBoundingBox`, `alignElements`, `calculateTranslation`, and `distributeElements`. It uses bounded owned axis-aligned/point-extrema bounds and mutation adapters, with no-op bound-text/binding updates. Geometry projections are the comparison scope; original rendering, binding updates, frame membership, version/timestamp mutation semantics and complete actions/GUI are excluded. Original action sources are read for predicate/axis/position/capture semantics, not executed. Each original call records input, selected parameters and output. All original source slices are authenticated and span/digest-bound.

The released `getMaximumGroups` deliberately uses the same map key space for an ungrouped element's ID and a group's ID (groups.ts lines 335–365). A collision therefore merges them in that helper. The collision fixture will preserve this actual behavior without assuming that a safer namespace distinction is automatically original-equivalent. Released negative-gap distribution sorts by centers but locates bounding-edge anchors separately and increments the center cursor only for nonanchors; overlap cases must compare that exact unusual rule.

This is work-only finite compatibility review preparation, with no production/public mutations, original GUI, full-suite or benchmark execution. Full native bindings and opaque mixed-group movement remain outside the declared candidate scope and must refuse atomically.
